NMR Spectroscopy. Samples for NMR analysis of metallopeptide complexes $\mathbf{4-6}$ were prepared by separately dissolving the linear peptides 1 $6.0\mathrm{mg}$ , $4.54\mu\mathrm{mol}\AA,$ , 2 ( ${\mathrm{6.0~mg}}$ , $3.16\mu\mathrm{mol}$ ), and 3 ( $6.0\mathrm{mg}$ , $3.26\mu\mathrm{mol})$ ) in $0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF- $d_{7}$ and titrating (directly into the NMR tube) the desired number of equivalents (2, 2, and 3 equiv for 1, 2, and 3, respectively) of Pd (from a stock solution of $\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{en})(\mathrm{ONO}_{2})_{2}$ : 9.48 mg, $32.6\ \mu\mathrm{mol}$ in $100~\mu\mathrm{L}$ of DMF- $d_{7}$ ). Titrations in DMF were monitored by recording 1D $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ -spectra immediately after Pd addition. Samples in water were prepared by dissolving the linear peptides 1 $\mathrm{4.0\mg}$ , $3.03\mu\mathrm{mol}\cdot$ ), 2 (4.0 mg, $2.11\mu\mathrm{mol}$ ), and 3 $(4.0\mathrm{mg},2.17\mu\mathrm{mol})$ in $0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of $_{\mathrm{H_{2}O/D_{2}O}}$ (9:1) and titrating (directly into the NMR tube) the desired number of equivalents (2, 2, and 3 equiv for 1, 2, and 3, respectively) of Pd (from a stock solution of $\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{en})(\mathrm{ONO}_{2})$ : $1.5~\mathrm{mg}$ , $5.00\mu\mathrm{mol}$ in $100\mu\mathrm{L}$ of $_{\mathrm{H_{2}O/D_{2}O}}$ (9:1)). The solutions were adjusted from $\mathrm{pH}2$ to $\mathrm{pH}4.2\mathrm{-}4.4$ using $0.01\mathrm{~M~NaOH}$ and $0.01\mathrm{~M~HNO_{3}}$ .
1D and 2D $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectra were recorded on either a Bruker ARX500 or Bruker Avance DMX-750 spectrometer. 2D $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ -spectra were recorded in phase-sensitive mode using time-proportional phase incrementation for quadrature detection in the $t1$ dimension.22a The 2D experiments included TOCSY (standard Bruker mlevtp or mlevph pulse programs) and NOESY (standard Bruker noesytp or noesyph pulse programs). TOCSY spectra were acquired over $6887~\mathrm{Hz}$ with 4096 complex data points in $F_{2}$ , 512 increments in $F_{1}$ , and 16 scans per increment. NOESY spectra were acquired over $^{6887~\mathrm{Hz}}$ with 4096 complex data points in $F_{2}$ , 512 increments in $F_{1}$ , and 48 scans per increment. TOCSY spectra were acquired with an isotropic mixing time of $80\mathrm{ms}$ . For TOCSY and NOESY experiments in $90\%$ $\%\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{:}10\%$ ${\bf D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , water suppression was achieved using a modified WATERGATE sequence.22b For $\mathrm{1D~^{1}H}$ NMR spectra in water, the water resonance was suppressed by low-power irradiation during the relaxation delay (1.5 s). Spectra were processed using XWINNMR (Bruker, Germany) software. The $t1$ dimensions of all 2D spectra were zero-filled to 1024 real data points with $90^{\circ}$ phase-shifted QSINE bell window functions applied in both dimensions followed by Fourier transformation and fifthorder polynomial baseline correction. $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ chemical shifts were referenced to the residual CHO proton in DMF- $d_{7}$ ( $\delta\ 8.01\ \mathrm{ppm})$ and to HDO $(\delta\ 4.80\ \mathrm{ppm})$ or 1,4-dioxane $\dot{\delta}3.70\ \mathrm{ppm},$ ) in water. $^3J_{\mathrm{NHCHa}}$ coupling constants were measured from high-resolution 1D spectra.
$^{15}\mathrm{N}$ NMR spectra $(40.5\mathrm{MHz})$ ) were obtained at $298\mathrm{K}$ using a DEPT pulse sequence on a Bruker AMX-400 spectrometer fitted with a $5\mathrm{-mm}$ broad-band tunable probe. $^{15}\mathrm{N}$ chemical shifts were referenced to the $^{15}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{4}{}^{+}$ signal $(0.0\ \mathrm{ppm})$ from $5~\mathrm{M}$ $\mathrm{(^{15}N H_{4})_{2}S O_{4}}$ in $1\mathrm{~M~H_{2}S O_{4}}$ in a coaxial capillary.
Mass Spectrometry. Positive ion electrospray mass spectra (ESIMS) were recorded on a Micromass (Manchester, U.K.) LCT-QTOF mass spectrometer. Samples for analysis of the metallopeptide complexes $\mathbf{4-6}$ were prepared in a manner identical to those used for NMR analysis (see above), except for the use of unlabeled DMF in place of DMF- $d_{7}$ . The DMF solutions of the complexes were injected directly into the injector port of the MS and carried to the source by a 1:1 mixture of solvents A and B (solvent $\mathrm{A}=100\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with $0.1\%$ formic acid; solvent $\mathbf{B}=90\%$ $\mathrm{CH_{3}C N:10\%}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with $0.1\%$ formic acid) at a flow rate of $100\ \mu\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . The mass spectra were recorded and processed with Masslynx software (version 3.5). The following isotopic distribution diagnostic for Pd was used in the analysis: $1.0\%$ $^{102}\mathrm{Pd}$ , $11.1\%$ $^{104}\mathrm{Pd}$ , $22.3\%$ $^{105}\mathrm{Pd}$ , $27.3\%$ $^{106}\mathrm{Pd}$ , $26.5\%$ $^{108}\mathrm{{Pd}}$ , and $11.8\%$ 110- Pd. To simplify the assignment of peaks, the $m/z$ values quoted in the text refer to the average mass of each peak cluster. These average masses were calculated from the average molecular weights (M) derived from the molecular formula. For example, in complex 4, the molecular formula $\left(\mathbf{C}_{62}\mathbf{H}_{103}\mathbf{N}_{23}\mathbf{O}_{17}\mathbf{P}\mathbf{d}_{2}\right)$ produces an average molecular weight $M$ $=1655.49$ amu. Therefore the $m/z=\left(\mathbf{M}/4\right)$ peak was assigned a value of $1655.49/4=413.9$ amu. The $m/z=(\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{H}^{+})/3$ peak was assigned a value of $(1655.59-1.00794)/3=551.5$ amu.
NMR Structure Calculations by Simulated Annealing and Energy Minimization. Structural Restraints. The distance restraints used in calculating the solution structures for $\mathbf{4-6}$ in DMF- $.d_{7}$ were derived from NOESY spectra (recorded at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ for 4 and 5 and 318 K for ${\bf6}$ ) using mixing times of $250~\mathrm{ms}$ for 5 or $350~\mathrm{{ms}}$ for 4 and 6 in water, and $300~\mathrm{{ms}}$ for 5 and $400~\mathrm{{ms}}$ for 4 and 6 in DMF. NOE crosspeak volumes were classified manually as strong (upper distance constraint ${\leq}2.7\mathrm{\AA}$ ), medium $(\leq3.5\mathrm{\AA})$ , weak $(\leq5.0\ \bar{\mathrm{\AA}})$ , and very weak $(\leq6.0\mathring\mathrm{A})$ , and standard pseudoatom distance corrections23 were applied for nonstereospecifically assigned protons. To address the possibility of conformational averaging, intensities were classified conservatively, and only upper distance limits were included in the calculations to allow the largest possible number of conformers to fit the experimental data. Backbone dihedral angle restraints were inferred from $^3J_{\mathrm{NHCHa}}$ coupling constants. For $^3J_{\mathrm{NHCHa}}\leq6~\mathrm{Hz}$ , $\phi$ was restrained to $-65\pm25^{\circ}$ . No restraints were included for residues with $6\leq\phi\leq8.5\mathrm{{Hz}}$ because of the problem of multiple solutions to the Karplus equation over this range.24 No residues in complexes 4, 5, or 6 displayed $^3J_{\mathrm{NHCHQ}}$ coupling constants ${>}8.5\operatorname{Hz}$ . No evidence for cis-peptide amides (i.e., no $\mathrm{CH}_{\mathrm{a}}-$ $\mathrm{CH}_{\upalpha}$ i, $i+1$ NOEs) was present in the NOESY spectra for the complexes, and therefore all $\omega$ -angles were set to trans $\mathit{\Omega}_{\leftmoon}\omega=180^{\circ},$ . Structures were calculated without any explicit hydrogen bond restraints to prevent structure biasing.
Structure Calculations. Three-dimensional structures of the peptide backbones in complexes $\mathbf{4-6}$ were calculated (without $\operatorname{Pd}(\mathrm{en})^{2+})$ using a dynamic simulated annealing and energy minimization protocol in the program X-PLOR 3.851. Starting structures with randomized $\phi$ and $\psi$ angles and extended side chains were generated using an ab initio simulated annealing protocol.25 The calculations were performed using the standard force field parameter set (PARALLHDG.PRO) and topology file (TOPALLHDG.PRO) in X-PLOR, which included inhouse modifications to account for histidine N3-methyl groups. Refinement of the structures was achieved using the conjugate gradient Powell algorithm with 1000 cycles of energy minimization and a refined force field based on the program $\mathrm{CHARMm^{26}}$ The final 20 structures chosen to represent the lowest energy conformation for each of $\mathbf{4-6}$ contained no distance violations $\geq0.2\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and no dihedral violations ${\geq}2^{\circ}$ . Structures were displayed in Insight II (version 2000.1, Accelrys, San Diego, CA).
The remaining two tripeptide conformations differ for the two solvents, since the components are taken from the separate preliminary NAMFIS evaluations of $\mathbf{7-9}$ (see below). Thus, conformation 5 is a combination of the top conformers derived from the three individual NAMFIS Pd-pentapeptide deconvolutions. In DMF- $\cdot d_{6}$ , the component represented by 7 is an ideal $\mathfrak{a}$ -helix; 8, a $\gamma$ -turn; and 9, a loop structure $\mathbf{N_{DMF}}$ , Figure 12a). In ${\bf D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , the corresponding fragments are a $\beta$ -turn, an ideal $\mathfrak{a}$ -helix, and an alternative loop structure $\bf N_{D20}$ , Figure 12b). In each case, the conformations represent $40-95\%$ of the total population of the peptide in solution. Conformation 6 is the top NAMFIS conformation from 5 optimized without constraints. For DMF$d_{6}$ , this structure is the least organized of the six and characterized by only three hydrogen bonds, none of which are found in the HELTH fragment $\mathbf{N_{DMF}^{\prime}}$ , Figure 12c). In ${\bf D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , the conformer sustains five hydrogen bonds, one of which is a side-chain backbone interaction $\bf{N}_{D20}^{\prime}$ , Figure 12d). The structure represents a modest reorganization of hydrogen bonds and a significant variation in backbone torsions relative to $\bf N_{D20}$ .
Each of the eight metalated tripeptide structures are depicted with hydrogen bonds in Figures 11 and 12. This small dataset allows evaluation of how well the ideal $\upalpha$ -helix conformation competes against alternative structures with diminishing helical character in the NAMFIS framework. The situation is not equivalent to a conformer deconvolution based on a complete or extensive search of conformational space as was performed for the individual pentapeptides $\mathbf{7-9}$ . Compound 6 is simply too complex to explore all important aspects of its conformational surface. However, the approach does bypass the assumption associated with a single target conformation in the simulated annealing exercise.
Circular Dichroism Spectra. CD measurements were performed using a Jasco model J-710 spectropolarimeter, which was routinely calibrated with (1S)- $(+)$ -10-camphorsulfonic acid. Each metallopeptide complex was dissolved in water (concentration $20{-}50\mu\mathrm{M})$ at different pH values ranging from 2 to 7. Temperature control was achieved using a Neslab RTE-111 circulating water bath. Spectra were recorded at $298\mathrm{K}$ , with a 0.1-cm Jasco quartz cell over the wavelength range $300-$ $190\mathrm{nm}$ at $50\ \mathrm{nm/min}$ with a bandwidth of $1.0\mathrm{nm}$ , response time of 2 s, resolution step width of $0.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and sensitivity of 20 M/deg. Each spectrum represents the average of five scans with smoothing to reduce noise.
## Results and Discussion
1D ${\bf\Pi}^{\mathbf{1}}\mathbf{H}$ NMR Spectroscopy. Formation of complexes $\mathbf{4-6}$ was identified by monitoring the reactions of $\mathrm{[Pd(en)(NO_{3})_{2}]}$ with $_{1-3}$ , respectively, in DMF- $d_{7}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ by 1D $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectroscopy. Aliquots of a stock solution of $\mathrm{[Pd(en)(NO_{3})_{2}]}$ in DMF- $\cdot d_{7}$ were titrated directly into NMR tubes containing solutions of free peptides $_{1-3}$ in DMF- $d_{7}$ . For peptides 1 and 2, addition of $^{<2}$ equiv of Pd produced complex spectra (Figures S1 and S2, Supporting Information), consistent with the presence of multiple species, but upon reaching 2.0 equiv, the complex myriad of signals rapidly converged to a single set of resonances, indicating just one thermodynamically stable metallopeptide product.
Similarly, titration of 3 with $\mathrm{[Pd(en)(NO_{3})_{2}]}$ in DMF- $d_{7}$ gave a complex mixture of species (Figure 1) with up to 3.0 equiv of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ , whereupon a single resonance set for just one species (6) was observed. However, the temperature needed to be raised to $318\mathrm{~K~}$ to ensure complete reaction after 3.0 equiv of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ . During this conversion, resonances for 6 in the amide/aromatic region of the spectrum $(\sim6.6\mathrm{-}9.5~\mathrm{ppm})$ were broadened and significantly overlapped, but on completion of the reaction (minutes at $318\ \mathrm{K}$ , the amide and aromatic resonances of 6 became sharper and fully resolved (Figure 1).
Mass Spectrometry. Although the 1D- (above) and 2D(below) $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra provided very strong evidence for the identity and structures of complexes $\mathbf{4-6}$ , we sought confirmation for their respective molecular masses from mass spectrometry. Pd contains six naturally occurring isotopes, so that each Pd species in mass spectra exhibits a complex cluster of isotope peaks. The separation between isotopes can be used to establish the charge state of the parent ion. For a $^{1+}$ ion, the isotopes are separated by 1 amu, 0.5 amu for a $^{2+}$ ion, 0.33 amu for a $^{3+}$ ion, etc. Acquiring positive ion electrospray mass spectra of peptides usually involves protonating some basic species, such as a free amine or amide, to generate a detectable ion. However, complexes $\mathbf{4-6}$ already contain $^{4+}$ , $^{4+}$ , and $^{6+}$ charges due to the presence of 2 or $3{\mathrm{Pd}}^{2+}$ ions and thus do not require protonation to be observed. One might therefore expect to see $m/z=(\mathbf{M}/4)$ ions for 4 and 5 and $m/z=(\mathbf{M}/6)$ ion for 6. Figure 2a,b (and Table 1) indeed show the $m/z=(\mathbf{M}/4)$ ions for both 4 and 5.
These were not the only species; two additional peaks were observed in each spectrum. The isotope separations within these extra peaks corresponded to $^{3+}$ and $^{2+}$ ions (Table 1), and for both complexes, the $m/z$ ratios for the two extra peaks corresponded to $(\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{H}^{+})/3)$ ) and $(\mathrm{M}-2\mathrm{H}^{+})/2)$ ions. When the three ions in each spectrum were deconvoluted, the reconstructed molecular mass (using Masslynx software, insets to Figure 2a,b) and isotope distribution corresponded to the theoretical $^{1+}$ molecular ion mass and distribution calculated for the molecular formulas of 4 and 5. This confirmed that all three ions corresponded to the expected different charge states for each of the two complexes 4 and 5. We suspect that the $^{3+}$ and $^{2+}$ ions arose from deprotonation of the Glu and Asp side chains in the mass spectrometer.
The mass spectrum for 6 was different in that the expected $m/z=(\mathbf{M}/6)$ ion was not observed. Only ions resulting from one, two, and three deprotonations, $(\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{H}^{+})/5$ , $(\mathbf{M}-2\mathbf{H}^{+})/$ 4, and $(\mathrm{M}-3\mathrm{H}^{+})/3$ , respectively, were observed. When these three ions were deconvoluted in a molecular mass reconstruction (Figure 2c inset), the resultant mass and isotope distribution corresponded to the expected theoretical $^{1+}$ molecular ion for $\mathrm{C_{87}H_{148}N_{34}O_{22}P d_{3}}$ , confirming that all three ions were simply different charge states of 6.
$\mathbf{CH}_{\mathbf{\mathfrak{a}}}$ Chemical Shifts. The $\mathrm{CH}_{\mathrm{a}}$ proton chemical shifts for residues in peptides and proteins are highly sensitive to structural effects. For residues involved in $\beta\mathrm{.}$ -strand or extended conformations, these chemical shifts tend to appear $>+0.1$ ppm downfield relative to their shifts when involved in random coils. For residues involved in $\mathbf{\alpha}\propto$ -helices, the $\mathrm{CH}_{\upalpha}$ shifts move upfield by similar amounts.30 NOESY spectra $(500\mathrm{MHz}\$ ) for free peptides $_{1-3}$ in DMF- $\cdot d_{7}$ (Figures S8-S10, Supporting Information) showed only intraresidue and sequential cross-peaks, with no medium or long-range cross-peaks, suggesting that $_{1-3}$ predominantly adopt randomly extended conformations in this solvent. Chemical shifts for $\mathrm{CH}_{\mathrm{a}}$ protons of free peptides $_{1-3}$ therefore approximate random coil values for the constituent amino acids in DMF- $d_{7}$ and can be compared directly with those of the corresponding complexes $\mathbf{4-6}$ as a measure of structure induction. Figure 4 shows that $\mathrm{CH}_{\upalpha}$ chemical shifts for $\mathbf{4-6}$ (except A3 and A4 of 6) are ${>}0.1\ \mathrm{ppm}$ upfield of those for corresponding free peptides $_{1-3}$ , supporting $\mathfrak{a}$ -helical structures for $\mathbf{4-6}$ . Upfield shifts of ${<}0.1\ \mathrm{ppm}$ for A3 and A4 of 6 may reflect some helicity within the AAA segment of 3, a feature supported by $^3J_{\mathrm{NHCHa}}$ coupling constant data below, as the helixstabilizing propensity of Ala-containing and poly-Ala sequences is well-known.31 Importantly, this helix induction is not due to aggregation as reflected by the fact that the upfield $\mathrm{CH}_{\upalpha}$ proton chemical shifts are independent of concentration, as shown for 4 versus 1 in DMF- $\cdot d_{7}$ at 0.1, 0.5, 1.0, and $10\mathrm{mM}$ concentrations (Figure S12, Supporting Information), and CD spectra were also unchanged for $100\mu\mathrm{M}$ and $30\mu\mathrm{M}$ for 4 in water.
$^{3}J_{\mathrm{NHCHd}}$ Coupling Constants. The magnitude of the $^3J_{\mathrm{NHCHa}}$ coupling constants observed for each residue in peptides (and proteins) is dependent upon the $\phi$ -angles (according to the Karplus equation)29,32 and therefore on local conformations in the polypeptide chains. Residues involved in secondary structures including $\beta$ -sheets and $\mathfrak{a}$ -helices can be identified from these coupling constants, since $^3J_{\mathrm{NHCHG}}$ values fall in the range of $4{-}6\operatorname{Hz}$ $(-70<\phi<-30)$ for $\mathfrak{a}$ -helices, $8{-}10\ \mathrm{Hz}$ $\cdot-150^{\circ}$ $<\phi<-90^{\circ};$ for $\beta$ -sheet conformations, and $6{-}8\ \mathrm{Hz}$ for random coil conformations.33 A series of three or more coupling constants ${<}6~\ \mathrm{Hz}$ is usually taken as evidence for helical structure.34
Amide Proton Temperature Coefficients. The magnitude of temperature dependence of the chemical shift for an amide NH is generally accepted as a measure of the degree to which it is protected from solvation, most often due to hydrogen bonding.35 For peptides, temperature coefficients $(\Delta\delta/T)\le3-4$ ppb/K are consistent with involvement in an intrachain hydrogen bond such as in an $\mathfrak{a}$ -helix or $\beta$ -sheet. Non-hydrogen-bonded peptide NHs exposed to solvent tend to display higher $\Delta\delta/T$ values. Free peptides $_{1-3}$ hardly show any amide proton temperature coefficients $\leq3-4$ (Figure 5 and Supporting Information), consistent with their assigned random conformations. In contrast, the majority of amide protons in the metallopeptides $\mathbf{4-6}$ display values in this range, consistent with their involvement in multiple $\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{O}(i)\to\mathrm{NH}(i+4)$ hydrogen bonds that stabilize $\mathbf{\ensuremath{a}}$ -helices.
NOE Distance Information. The presence of NOE crosspeaks in 2D-NOESY spectra of peptides usually provides the most convincing evidence of structure in solution, since they are the least subject to factors that often complicate the interpretation of other spectral data.36 Importantly, they allow conformational analysis on a per residue basis. Summaries of the sequential and medium range NOE information for metallopeptide complexes $\mathbf{4-6}$ in DMF are presented in Figure 5. Residues involved in $\mathfrak{a}$ -helices are connected throughout by short $d_{\upalpha\mathrm{N}}(i,i+1)$ , $d_{\mathrm{NN}}(i,i+1)$ , $d_{\upalpha\mathrm{N}}(i,i+3)$ , $d_{\upalpha\mathrm{N}}(i,i+4)$ , and $d_{\upalpha\beta}(i,i+3)$ distances and to a lesser extent by $d_{\mathrm{oN}}(i,i+$ 2) distances.36a Figure 5 shows that NOE cross-peaks corresponding to these short distances dominate the NOESY spectra of complexes $\mathbf{4-6}$ , the $(i,i+3)$ , and especially $(i,i+4)$ correlations, providing very strong evidence of $\mathfrak{a}$ -helical structures for $\mathbf{4-6}$ .
Solution Structures in DMF- $d_{7}$ by Simulated Annealing. Calculating 3D solution structures for short peptides from NMRderived interproton distances and torsion angle restraints is often perilous because it assumes the existence of a single set of related conformations. Single conformations are normal for globular proteins because numerous electrostatic, hydrophobic, and hydrogen-bonding interactions provide extensive conformational stabilization; therefore, standard simulated annealing37 and distance geometry38 protocols are ideal methods for revealing their structure. Short peptides, on the other hand, have few of these stabilizing interactions and consequently tend to exist as ensembles of rapidly interconverting, low-energy conformers. Because NMR parameters are collected as timeand ensemble-averaged quantities, the NOE and $J.$ -couplings for most peptides tend to correspond only to population-weighted averaged values, and single conformations calculated from such data may not necessarily reflect the overall solution structure, unless that conformation exists with a highly predominant statistical weight.39
As always, there are population-weighted averages of conformations for metallopeptides $\mathbf{4-6}$ but additional constraints, imposed by the two (for 4 and 5) and three (for 6) modules of $\mathrm{[Pd(en)(H^{\ast}X X X H^{\ast})]^{2+}}$ , which adopt stable helical turns on their own,17 are expected to significantly limit conformational freedom compared with free peptides, leading to only one or a few stable conformations. Indeed, the combined NMR data $\mathrm{{CH}_{\mathrm{{a}}}}$ chemical shifts, $^3J_{\mathrm{NHCHd}}$ coupling constants, VT-NMR data, NOE observations) overwhelmingly point to a predominance of $\mathfrak{a}$ -helical conformations for $\mathbf{4-6}$ , and therefore, the calculation of NMR-derived solution structures is well-justified here.
Structures for $\mathbf{4-6}$ in DMF- $d_{7}$ were calculated from the following NMR-derived restraints. For $4,234~\mathrm{NOE}$ distance restraints (105 intraresidue, 60 sequential, 69 medium range $i$ $\to i+2$ , $i\to i+3$ , or $i\rightarrow i+4\rangle$ and 9 backbone $\phi$ -dihedral angle restraints inferred from $^3J_{\mathrm{NHCHa}}$ coupling constants were used. For 5, 291 NOEs (109 intraresidue, 75 sequential, 107 medium range) and $14\phi$ -dihedral angles were used. For 6, 279 NOEs (124 intraresidue, 72 sequential, 83 medium range) and
14 $\phi$ -dihedral angles were used (see Table S7, Supporting Information). The structures were calculated in $\mathrm{XPLOR^{40}}$ using a dynamic simulated annealing protocol in a geometric force field and were energy-minimized using a modified $\mathrm{CHARMm}^{26}$ force field. No explicit hydrogen bond restraints were included in the structure calculations, even though the temperatureindependent amide NHs strongly support multiple H-bonds, to prevent exclusion of any non- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{a}$ -helical H-bonded conformers (e.g., $3_{10}$ -helices) should they fit the data.
Solution Structures for $\mathbf{4-6}$ in Water. Peptides $_{1-3}$ , dissolved in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (9:1), were also reacted in NMR tubes at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ with aliquots of $\mathrm{[Pd(^{15}N H_{2}C H_{2}C H_{2}^{15}N H_{2})(N O_{3})_{2}]}$ in $_{\mathrm{H_{2}O/D_{2}O}}$ (9:1) appropriate for anticipated conversion to complexes $\mathbf{4-6}$ . The solutions were adjusted to $\mathrm{pH}4.2\mathrm{-}4.4$ , using $0.01\mathrm{MNaOH}$ and $0.01\mathrm{M}\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ , and $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR spectra revealed formation of a single species in each case.
Unlike DMF solutions, circular dichroism spectra (Figure 8) could be measured for the peptides in water to corroborate our assertion of higher structure for 6 than for 4, although there was some absorption by the metal moiety. Peptides longer than 15 residues that are $\mathfrak{a}$ -helical typically give prominent double minima (222, 208 nm) and a large positive molar ellipticity at $190~\mathrm{nm}$ . The relative percent helicity can theoretically be quantitated for long peptides by the mean residue ellipticity (MRE) at $222~\mathrm{nm}$ .42 Although shorter peptides do not usually conform well to this relationship, we considered it worthwhile to calculate helicity at $222{\mathrm{nm}}$ for free and metal-bound peptides to give a rough guide as to their rank order of helicity. Using the mean residue elipticity (MRE) at $222~\mathrm{nm}$ and recognizing that this method of quantifying helicity probably gives an underestimate for short peptides, we calculated42 peptide helicity as 1-3 $(<5\%)$ , 4 $(21\%)$ , and 6 $(41\%)$ .
Thus, the free peptides have negligible structure ( $<5\%$ helicity) in water, consistent also with their minima ${\sim}195- $ $200~\mathrm{nm}$ , which is typical of random structures in water. The 10-residue peptide 4 and 15-residue peptide 6, possessing “back to back” metal clips, exhibited spectra expected for helical peptides. The MRE ratio $[\theta]_{222}/[\theta]_{208}$ is sometimes a useful indicator of the nature of the helix, being ${\sim}0.7{-}1$ for $\mathfrak{a}$ -helicity but lower $(\sim0.4\mathrm{-}0.7)$ for $3_{10}$ -helicity.43 We find $[\theta]_{222}/[\theta]_{208}$ ${\sim}0.99$ for 6 and ${\sim}0.53$ for 4, suggesting possibly a greater proportion of $3_{10}$ -helix in the conformational mix for 4 compared with a higher $\mathfrak{a}$ -helix content for 6.
Figure 9 summarizes coupling constants $^3J_{\mathrm{NHCHa}}$ $(\leq6~\mathrm{Hz})$ ), amide NH temperature coefficients $\Delta\delta/T\left(\leq4\mathrm{ppb}/\mathrm{K}\right)$ , and NOE correlations (sequential and medium range) for the in situ reaction products $\mathbf{4-6}$ in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (9:1). For all three compounds there are fewer low coupling constants and small temperature coefficients (Table S2, Supporting Information) than observed in DMF (Figure 5), and in particular, there are fewer $i\rightarrow i+3$ and $i\rightarrow i+4$ NOE correlations. Also, the $\mathrm{CH}_{\upalpha}$ chemical shift differences between $_{1-3}$ and $\mathbf{4-6}$ (Supporting
Information) are consistent $\ \Delta\delta<0$ to $-0.1\ \mathrm{ppm}$ ) with more helical structure in ${\bf6}>{\bf4}>{\bf5}$ , and this seems to suggest that the intervening residues in 5 between the metal clips (AVTDY)
are not highly helix-favoring in water. We concluded from all these data that there is much less structure for $\mathbf{4-6}$ in water than in DMF, with possibly sufficient structure in 6 alone in water to permit a structure calculation.
The calculated lowest energy solution structures for 6 in water are displayed in Figure 10a and compared with the lowest energy average structure of 6 in DMF (Figure 10b). The calculated structure is $\mathbf{\alpha}\propto$ -helical, consistent with the characteristic NMR paramaters and CD spectra described above, with four helical turns in evidence. However, the family of lowest energy structures is less superimposable for water (Figure 10a) than for DMF (Figure 5c), and in comparing the lowest energy structure in each family (Figure 10b), the water structure is clearly skewed relative to the DMF structure due to elongation and kinking. The NOE summary diagrams (Figures 5 and 9) show a smaller ratio of $(i\rightharpoonup i+4)/(i\rightharpoonup i+2)$ NOEs in the protic solvent water than in the aprotic solvent DMF, consistent with less peptide structure because of greater competition from water for the backbone amide carbonyls. In other words, there are fewer intramolecular helix-defining H-bonds and less $a\cdot$ - character in water than DMF, reflecting greater contributions to the overall structure by more heavily populated alternate conformations such as the $3_{10}$ -helix. This agrees with the CD spectral results above.
Solution Structures by NAMFIS Analysis. A second approach to determination of structure in solution is NAMFIS. Unlike the simulated annealing method which focuses all NMR variables (NOE distances and ${}^{3}J.$ -related dihedral angles) on calculating the averaged lowest energy structures, NAMFIS parses the same variables among the members of a collection of conformations in rapid equilibrium.27,28 The method deconvolutes an averaged NMR spectrum into contributing components, identifies individual conformational minima, and assigns weights to the participating conformers. Thus, the burden of accommodating the complete set of averaged data is shared among $5-20$ unstrained, low-energy conformers. This assumes, of course, that a mixture of conformers, and not a single structure, properly describes the problem in question. However, were a single unique conformation the only one present in solution, NAMFIS would assign a particularly high weight to it. For compound 6, we are dealing with three concatenated 22- membered ring cyclic pentapeptides, each of which is characterized by considerable conformational mobility (see below). It is expected that $_{10-15}$ -mers such as $\mathbf{4-6}$ exist as a small collection of mobile forms despite the three locally rigid Pd clips. Accordingly, we have examined the solution structure of the metalated peptide 6 with NAMFIS, an approach belonging to a family of methods designed to address the multipleconformation problem.44
For analysis of tri-Pd peptide 6 in DMF- $d_{6}$ , all NMR parameters $(279~\mathrm{\NOEs}$ and $15^{3}J_{\mathrm{NHCHa}}^{3}\mathrm{s})$ were used by NAMFIS, with the set of conformers limited to six structures: the ideal $\upalpha$ -helix, $\mathbf{H_{DMF}}$ , the $a-$ and (3,10)-mixed helices $\mathbf{H^{\prime}}_{\mathbf{DMF}}$ , $\mathbf{H^{\prime\prime}}_{\mathbf{DMF}}$ , and $\mathbf{H^{\prime\prime\prime}}_{\mathbf{DMF}}$ (Figure 11), and ideal NAMFIS conformers $\mathbf{N_{DMF}}$ and $\mathbf{N^{\prime}}_{\mathbf{DMF}}$ (Figure 12a,b). NAMFIS matching of the NMR-determined variables against the set of six conformations delivers one main contributor to the solution ensemble: $\mathbf{H^{\prime\prime}}_{\mathbf{DMF}}$ $(92\%)$ . The remaining $8\%$ is composed of $\mathbf{H^{\prime\prime\prime}}_{\mathbf{DMF}}$ , NDMF, and $\mathbf{N_{DMF}^{\prime}}$ . Thus, the major form predicted for 6 in DMF- $d_{6}$ is the optimized ideal $\mathfrak{a}$ -helix with backbone i, $i+4$ hydrogen bonds extending the length of the helix, except for peptide fragment 9, which presents as a $3_{10}$ -helix. Accordingly, the predominant structure obtained from NAMFIS treatment of the DMF data (Figure 11, $\mathbf{H_{DMF}})$ ) is in very close agreement with the simulated annealing derived structure for 6 depicted in Figures 6c and 10.
Tripeptide 6 shows 251 NOEs and $11~{^3J_{\mathrm{NHCHa}}}$ ’s in ${\bf D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The “best fit” of the data against the six conformers noted above results in the contribution of two main forms: $\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{\mathbf{\mathbf{\mathbf{\mathbf{\deltau}}}}}2\mathbf{\mathbf{\mathbf{0}}}}^{\prime}$ $(73\%)$ and $\mathbf{N}_{\mathbf{D2O}}^{\prime}(27\%)$ . The idealized $\mathfrak{a}$ -helix contributes a diminutive $0.5\%$ to the equilibrium population. Nonetheless, the lead conformer is composed of 70/30 R-helix $/3_{10}$ -helix, while the second equilibrium partner sustains $20\mathrm{-}30\%$ $\upalpha$ -helical character. The combination is semiquantitatively consistent with the CD estimate for 6 that $41\%$ is a lower boundary for peptide helicity. It furthermore supports the conclusion above that there is more helicity in DMF than in $\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .
## Conclusions
The electrophilic $\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{en})^{2+}$ has been found to be a remarkably regioselective peptide-binding probe or “metal clip” that is also helix-inducing. Coordination to three $_{10-15}$ residue peptides possessing a number of potential metal-binding donors, including $_{4-6}$ histidines in a variety of positions, was highly regioselective, with the thermodynamically stable products bearing 22-membered metal chelate rings each involving $(i,i+4)$ histidines coordinated via imidazole N1 to $\operatorname{Pd}(\operatorname{II})$ . This stable macrocycle $\mathrm{[Pden(HxxxH)l^{2+}}$ is closely analogous to metal-bound regions in metalloproteins, but quite unlike reported short peptide binding modes in small inorganic complexes, where five- and six-membered rings predominate. We attribute the regioselective $(i,i+4)$ coordination and the resultant stability of the 22-membered ring to unique presentation of $i$ , $i+4$ His residues on the same peptide face, as occurs in a helical turn, the proximity enabling chelation to cis-positions on the $\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{en})^{2+}$ moiety.
The observations of much greater helical structure in the aprotic dipolar solvent DMF than in the protic solvent water may be very relevant for proteins, where this and other HxxxH sequences tend to be buried in quite hydrophobic environments rather than located on water-exposed protein surfaces. Our use of the N3-methyl histidines here, to simplify NMR analysis, does not detract from the fact that the species we see are also accessible by unsubstituted His residues as previously reported.17a The HxxxH signature is extremely common both in metalloproteins and in proteins not previously associated with metal ions. This work supports the possibility that metal ions could have important templating roles during protein folding. It is conceivable that even transient coordination of labile (first row transition) metal ions could template helix-folding. Once helices become sufficiently long, or when helix-stabilizing hydrophobic protein environments become created through folding, the metal template may no longer be required to preserve the thermodynamic stability of a helix and could be displaced from some proteins by other metal-binding ligands. Roles for metal-induced helix formation in peptides and proteins should certainly be investigated further.
